/E (significantly towards zero as the eyelids move closer together, indicating a reduction in the eye's visible area. This decrease in EAR is a clear sign of a blink, which typically lasts only a fraction of a second. As the blink ends and the eyelids return to their normal open position, the EAR rapidly increases back to its original value. This dynamic change in the EAR is a valuable signal that can be used to detect and track blinks in real-time, which is particularly important for detecting drowsiness in drivers or individuals in critical environments.)
